<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shatt al-Arab</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arvand Rud</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shatt al-Arab is the river formed by the joining of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, flowing past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. Its lower course, where the Karun joins it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Khorramshahr</a>, forms part of the border between Iraq and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Its marshes and canals sheltered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a> rebels in the 9th century, and its control was a cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war">Iran–Iraq War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab.jpg" alt="Shatt al-Arab" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aziz1005 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shat_al-arab-22.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Shatt al-Arab, Arabic for River of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, is about 200 km long. It forms where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> meet at al-Qurnah in the Basra Governorate and flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> south of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaryat-al-faw">al-Faw</a>. It is about 232 m wide at Basra and 800 m at its mouth. The Karun joins it from the Iranian side at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Khorramshahr</a> and deposits large amounts of silt, so that continuous dredging is needed to keep the river navigable. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> it is known as the Arvand Rud, the Valiant River. In the mid-1970s its banks held 17 to 18 million date palms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Control of the river was disputed in the age of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>. The Safavids gained most of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in the early 16th century, but lost it to the Ottomans after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-amasya">Peace of Amasya</a> in 1555. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Abbas I</a> recovered the river, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zuhab">Treaty of Zuhab</a> in 1639 permanently ceded it to the Ottomans, although it never marked the southern frontier precisely. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> restored Iranian control for a time, until the Treaty of Kerden of 1746. Treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> in 1823 and 1847 and a protocol of 1913 again failed to settle the boundary.</p><p>In 1937 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> signed a treaty that placed the border at the low-water mark on the eastern bank, except at Abadan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Khorramshahr</a>, so that Iraq controlled almost the whole waterway; ships had to fly the Iraqi flag and take an Iraqi pilot, and Iran paid tolls. Iran abrogated the treaty in April 1969. In March 1975 the Algiers Accord fixed the border on a line close to the thalweg, the deepest channel, in exchange for an end to Iranian support for the Iraqi Kurds.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> abrogated that treaty in 1980 and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and the waterway was the scene of much of the fighting in the Iran-Iraq War, which lasted until 1988. When Iran captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaryat-al-faw">al-Faw</a> in 1986, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s shipping through the river virtually stopped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The southern part of the river forms the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> down to its mouth. It is Iraq's only outlet to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> is a major port for loading oil tankers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a>'s Bubiyan Island is part of its delta. By 2002 more than 14 million of the date palms had been destroyed by war, salt and pests.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shatt al-Arab” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The religion has been practised mainly around the lower Karun, Euphrates and Tigris and the rivers of the <b>Shatt al-Arab</b>, in southern Iraq and Khuzestan in Iran.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mandaeism">Mandaeism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The pieces were disassembled by a numbering system, packed in straw in coal barrels to disguise them, shipped down the Euphrates to the <b>Shatt al-Arab</b> and loaded onto German ships for Berlin.</blockquote><a href="/work/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The Muhaysin, a branch of the Banu Ka'b sheikhdom, developed the port of Mohammerah (modern Khorramshahr) at the confluence of the Karun and the <b>Shatt al-Arab</b> in the early 19th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-mohammerah">Sheikhdom of Mohammerah</a></li><li><blockquote>The rebels built a fortified capital, al-Mukhtara, among the canals of the <b>Shatt al-Arab</b> region.</blockquote><a href="/state/zanj-state">Zanj state</a></li></ul></section>
